      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Paweł Huryn</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/PawelHuryn">@PawelHuryn</a>)</p><blockquote><p>I ran 60 metered audits. The 2x-per-token model came out 4x cheaper.<br/><br/>Fable 5 vs Opus 4.8. 30 audits per model, same three files, every run billed from session traces. The job wasn't answering questions. It was finding problems, the work you'd actually delegate to an agent.<br/><br/>The price depends on the unit:<br/><br/>→ Per token: 2x. The sticker price.<br/>→ Per audit: 2.5x ($2.93 vs $1.17). Fable wrote more.<br/>→ Per finding: 1.25x. Fable surfaced 2x more issues.<br/>→ Per the bug that mattered: 0.25x.<br/><br/>A median of 14 findings against 7 could just mean more noise. That's why I planted bugs: known ground truth, caught or missed, no judgment calls.<br/><br/>The one that mattered was a contradiction between two files. Neither looked wrong on its own. Fable caught it in 20 of 30 audits. Opus caught it in 2. Expected spend to catch it once: $4.40 vs $17.55.<br/><br/>Before you ask what a token costs, ask what a miss costs. Volume subtasks where misses are cheap: the cheap model wins. Review work where one miss ships the bug: the deep model is the bargain. That's the split my CLAUDE.md encodes now.<br/><br/>You aren't buying tokens. You're buying the finding you'd have missed.</p></blockquote><div><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKoHqJWWUAAW3RA.jpg" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/></div><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 31 · 🔁 2 · 💬 3 · 🔖 39</p><p><a href="https://x.com/PawelHuryn/status/2065472022566649897">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
