## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKqc46UXIAA3Tka?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/article/2065625151383732422/media/2065635880644059136) How SIA's Self-Improving Loop Works, And How to Actually Run It [![Square profile picture](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2014100845189529600/Ff1Xc28-_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI) [AlphaSignal...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>AlphaSignal AI</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/AlphaSignalAI">@AlphaSignalAI</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065383150319280129">http://x.com/i/article/2065383150319280129</a></p></blockquote><div><img src="https://opengraph.githubassets.com/ecde4a8fda7ae681e953a8f6628803fe58b6df8aac049d4d2115a1631a47a8d0/NVIDIA/SkillSpector" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://camo.githubusercontent.com/d447d7d53910a2d345c72845df16d927da9d7cdf1063cd4861c3d0b5eefea808/68747470733a2f2f696d672e736869656c64732e696f2f62616467652f707974686f6e2d332e31322b2d626c75652e737667" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://camo.githubusercontent.com/a549a7a30bacba7bfceebdc207a8e86c3f2c02995a2527640dca30048fd2b64e/68747470733a2f2f696d672e736869656c64732e696f2f62616467652f4c6963656e73652d417061636865253230322e302d626c75652e737667" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/></div><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065383150319280129">http://x.com/i/article/2065383150319280129</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKnow09boAAzROg?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/article/2065441777591693430/media/2065437829673754624)<br/>The Skills You're Installing Into Your AI Agents Are Not Vetted <br/>[![Square profile picture](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2014100845189529600/Ff1Xc28-_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI)<br/>[AlphaSignal AI](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI)<br/>[@AlphaSignalAI](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI)<br/>·<br/>2h<br/>·<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow AlphaSignalAI<br/>1<br/>2<br/>8<br/>[450](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/status/2065441777591693430/analytics)<br/>## <br/>Agent skills add instructions, permissions, and tool access to your workflow. NVIDIA's SkillSpector checks whether they do what they claim.<br/>&gt; In 4 minutes, you'll learn why a skill can look safe in code review and still be dangerous in practice.<br/>Research found that 26.1% of published agent skills contain vulnerabilities and 5.2% show likely malicious intent.<br/>A separate Snyk study scanning 3,984 skills found 36% contained prompt injection vectors, with trojans, cryptominers, and credential harvesters in publicly available packages.<br/>Most of these pass traditional security checks.<br/>Every developer using Claude Code, Codex, or Gemini CLI has done the same thing.<br/>Found a skill that looked useful. Installed it. Moved on.<br/>Nobody scanned it. Nobody checked what it actually does. Nobody verified that what it says it does matches what it actually does.<br/>NVIDIA just open-sourced a purpose-built scanner for this problem. It's called SkillSpector.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKm4Sw0asAAUoto?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/article/2065441777591693430/media/2065384536607993856)<br/>## <br/>Why this is harder than scanning normal code<br/>You might think: code is code. Existing security scanners handle this.<br/>They don't. Not fully.<br/>A traditional security scanner looks at what code does. Does it make network calls? Does it access the filesystem? Does it contain known vulnerable dependencies?<br/>These are structural questions with structural answers.<br/>Agent skills introduce a different kind of risk. A skill can pass every structural check and still be dangerous. It operates with implicit trust inside an agent's reasoning loop.<br/>The threat surface is different from regular software in three ways.<br/>Prompt injection<br/>A skill's instruction content can contain hidden directives that hijack the agent's behavior. The code looks clean. The instructions redirect the agent.<br/>Intent mismatch<br/>A skill describes one capability in its metadata while its actual instructions enable something broader. The description says "summarize documents." The instructions also exfiltrate the content to an external endpoint.<br/>Excessive agency<br/>A skill requests permissions beyond what its declared purpose requires. Metadata claims narrow scope. Actual execution reaches further.<br/>None of these are caught by checking whether the code compiles or whether it calls known malicious URLs. They require understanding what the skill is trying to do, not just what it structurally contains.<br/>## <br/>The two-stage architecture<br/>SkillSpector runs two distinct analysis stages. The combination is what makes it useful.<br/>Stage 1: Static analysis<br/>Fast pattern matching across 64 vulnerability checks in 16 categories.<br/>This runs in seconds and catches the structural problems: dangerous code patterns via AST analysis, taint flow tracking, credential access paths, and MCP permission scope violations.<br/>The 16 categories cover both conventional software risks and agent-specific threats:<br/>Prompt injection, data exfiltration, privilege escalation, supply chain vulnerabilities, excessive agency, output handling issues, system prompt leakage, memory poisoning, tool misuse, rogue agent patterns, trigger abuse, dangerous code (AST), taint tracking, YARA signatures, MCP least privilege</blockquote><p>🔗 <a href="https://github.com/NVIDIA/SkillSpector/">GitHub - NVIDIA/SkillSpector: Security scanner for AI agent skills. Detect vulnerabilities, malicious patterns, and security risks. · GitHub</a></p><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065615613813288962">http://x.com/i/article/2065615613813288962</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKqc46UXIAA3Tka?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/article/2065625151383732422/media/2065635880644059136)<br/>How SIA's Self-Improving Loop Works, And How to Actually Run It <br/>[![Square profile picture](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2014100845189529600/Ff1Xc28-_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI)<br/>[AlphaSignal AI](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI)<br/>[@AlphaSignalAI](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI)<br/>·<br/>3h<br/>·<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow AlphaSignalAI<br/>1<br/>1<br/>5<br/>[483](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/status/2065625151383732422/analytics)<br/>A Feedback-Agent that rewrites scaffolds and trains LoRA weights, three SOTA results, an MIT repo, and a 10-minute setup<br/>&gt; In \~8 mins: the 3-agent loop, the harness-vs-weights ablation across 3 benchmarks, a 6-step install-to-run tutorial, the verifier contract for your own tasks, and the one place the public repo diverges from the paper.<br/>SIA just hit 70.1% Top-1 accuracy on LawBench's 191-class Chinese legal benchmark, where the prior best was 45.0%.<br/>Hexo Labs released it on May 28 as an MIT-licensed repo, alongside an arXiv paper titled "SIA: Self Improving AI with Harness &amp; Weight Updates" co-authored with the University of Oxford.<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1763090471822536704/l57OOjTj_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/kunalbhatia91)<br/>Kunal Bhatia<br/>@kunalbhatia91<br/>·<br/>[May 28](http://x.com/kunalbhatia91/status/2060013228231725092)<br/>Superintelligence will be built on Self Improvement. Today <br/>[@hexoai](http://x.com/hexoai)<br/>, we’re excited to release ‘SIA’ - an open-source Self-Improving AI, to achieve any goal through recursive self improvement. While trying to solve a problem, SIA doesn't just improve it's abilities by updating<br/>Show more<br/>![](https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2060003043698225152/img/HHSLcFI59c-w9fbq.jpg)<br/>121<br/>226<br/>638<br/>[516K](http://x.com/kunalbhatia91/status/2060013228231725092/analytics)<br/>The pitch: one loop where an AI rewrites the agent's scaffold and retrains the model's weights, two levers prior research only pulled separately.<br/>The catch: the paper's version picks the lever automatically. The version you can run today asks you to pick it yourself.<br/># <br/>What SIA actually is<br/>Every agent is two things glued together. The harness (system prompt, tools, parsers, retry logic) decides how the model works the problem, and the weights decide what the model knows.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKqSNLJWcAM4myD?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/article/2065625151383732422/media/2065624134130757635)<br/>Self-improvement research split along that seam. One school has a meta-agent rewrite the scaffold while the weights stay frozen, the other trains weights at test time while the scaffold stays frozen.<br/>SIA runs both levers in a single loop. When scaffold edits stop paying, it trains the model instead, using the task's own scoring function as the reward.<br/># <br/>Three benchmarks, one clean ablation<br/>The paper isolates each lever's contribution. SIA-H is harness updates only, SIA-W+H adds weight updates on top.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKqM-YIXMAAfGnt?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/article/2065625151383732422/media/2065618382360096768)<br/>The TriMul row translates to a 1,017 µs kernel against the prior SOTA's 1,161 µs, a 14.02x speedup over SIA's own baseline. Adding weight updates after the harness phase cut 91.9% off the harness-only peak of 12,483 µs.<br/>The most quotable result is two lines of NumPy. On the denoising task, the first weight-update checkpoint added an np.clip \+ np.rint step that rounds imputed gene counts to non-negative integers, a biological constraint no scaffold edit produced across every harness iteration.<br/>One caveat before you screenshot the table. All numbers come from the authors' own verifier setup (paper, Table 3), with no</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 19 · 🔁 4 · 💬 2 · 🔖 28</p><p><a href="https://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/status/2065441777591693430">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
      <media:content url="https://opengraph.githubassets.com/ecde4a8fda7ae681e953a8f6628803fe58b6df8aac049d4d2115a1631a47a8d0/NVIDIA/SkillSpector" medium="image" />
      <media:content url="https://camo.githubusercontent.com/d447d7d53910a2d345c72845df16d927da9d7cdf1063cd4861c3d0b5eefea808/68747470733a2f2f696d672e736869656c64732e696f2f62616467652f707974686f6e2d332e31322b2d626c75652e737667" medium="image" />
