## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKg8U-vagAA4jyK?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/hnshah/article/2065199404446068904/media/2064966760286289920) The 5-Minute AI Exercise I’d Run With Every Team [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1498191257218076672/rvtMDI8Q_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/hnshah) [Hiten...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Hiten Shah</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/hnshah">@hnshah</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064956828736249856">http://x.com/i/article/2064956828736249856</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064956828736249856">http://x.com/i/article/2064956828736249856</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKg8U-vagAA4jyK?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/hnshah/article/2065199404446068904/media/2064966760286289920)<br/>The 5-Minute AI Exercise I’d Run With Every Team<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1498191257218076672/rvtMDI8Q_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/hnshah)<br/>[Hiten Shah![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/65441891/crazyegg_logo_avatar_bigger.png)](http://x.com/hnshah)<br/>[@hnshah](http://x.com/hnshah)<br/>·<br/>12h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow hnshah<br/>1<br/>10<br/>104<br/>[21K](http://x.com/hnshah/status/2065199404446068904/analytics)<br/>The fastest way to find real AI leverage inside a company has very little to do with testing the latest model or rolling out another tool.<br/>Teams often focus on visible signs of progress. They share prompt libraries, schedule training sessions, connect new applications, and expand access across the organization. The goal is simple.<br/>Increase usage and hope it leads to better results. Yet the more valuable opportunity is usually hidden in the work itself. It appears in the small moments when people repeatedly improve AI outputs using their own judgment and experience.<br/>I’d start with a five-minute exercise.<br/>Ask everyone on the team one question.<br/>What is one AI correction you made more than once this week?<br/>That question cuts through the noise because it starts with real work. It skips the abstract AI conversation and goes straight to the moments where the model got close, someone with context stepped in, and the output became useful.<br/>Those moments are where the first useful AI skills usually hide.<br/>I’m walking through how to turn these repeated corrections into skills this Friday with Claude and ChatGPT. You can <br/>[join the free live session here](https://hiten.com/how-to-build-skills)<br/>.<br/>## <br/>Repeated Correction Is The Signal<br/>Every team using AI has a version of this problem.<br/>A model drafts a customer summary that includes the facts while missing the risk. Sales prep comes back with account history while ignoring the objection from the last call. A research recap lists the feedback without finding the pattern. A page sounds polished, although the claim still has no proof.<br/>Someone fixes the output and the work gets better. A few days later, the same kind of task shows up and the same correction happens again.<br/>That repeated correction is easy to dismiss as cleanup because the work already moved forward. The useful part is what the correction reveals.<br/>Someone on the team has a method the model does not have yet.<br/>## <br/>The Method Is The Asset<br/>A useful AI skill usually starts with a small frustration.<br/>You keep telling AI to separate facts from inference. Someone else keeps asking it to make the claim more specific. Another person keeps adding customer proof before copy gets polished. A manager keeps rewriting updates so they explain what matters instead of listing what happened.<br/>These corrections look tactical from the outside. Inside the company, they carry judgment.<br/>A strong salesperson can spot the real obstacle in a deal. An experienced support lead can tell when a conversation is heading toward escalation. A sharp marketer recognizes when a claim sounds compelling but lacks proof, while a product person can distinguish isolated feedback from a meaningful pattern.<br/>Companies already have these methods. They live in people’s heads, review comments, Slack threads, old docs, customer calls, and repeated conversations.<br/>AI makes them easier to see because the model keeps almost doing the work.<br/>Almost is the important word. When AI gets close and someone still has to fix it, the fix is worth studying.<br/>## <br/>The Exercise<br/>The exercise should feel simple enough to do during a team meeting.<br/>Ask three to five people what AI output they corrected more than once this week, then listen for the pattern underneath the answers.<br/>One person might</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 172 · 🔁 11 · 💬 2 · 🔖 482</p><p><a href="https://x.com/hnshah/status/2065199404446068904">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
