## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKd9q8_WoAA2R68?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/DinScales26/article/2064758924369166403/media/2064757131052490752) the behavioral science behind B2B sales cycles... [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2004607244667658240/Nca2FK2__normal.jpg)](http://x.com/DinScales26) [Din](http://x.com/DinScales26)...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Din</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/DinScales26">@DinScales26</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064543044607565824">http://x.com/i/article/2064543044607565824</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064543044607565824">http://x.com/i/article/2064543044607565824</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKd9q8_WoAA2R68?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/DinScales26/article/2064758924369166403/media/2064757131052490752)<br/>the behavioral science behind B2B sales cycles... <br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2004607244667658240/Nca2FK2__normal.jpg)](http://x.com/DinScales26)<br/>[Din](http://x.com/DinScales26)<br/>[@DinScales26](http://x.com/DinScales26)<br/>·<br/>23h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow DinScales26<br/>2<br/>3<br/>11<br/>[1.2K](http://x.com/DinScales26/status/2064758924369166403/analytics)<br/>b2b deals stall because of how the human brain handles RISK, especially risk shared across a group. <br/>once you understand the wiring underneath it, the cycle starts looking predictable,<br/>which means it finally looks like something you can move on purpose instead of something that just happens to you and you can't control...<br/>## <br/>the real competitor is doing nothing:<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKd9OUiXwAAkHM1?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/DinScales26/article/2064758924369166403/media/2064756639157174272)<br/>most reps think they're competing against other vendors. <br/>they build battle cards against the three obvious rivals and rehearse why they win the bake off. <br/>BUT, the actual competitor in nearly every b2b deal is the status quo. doing nothing<br/>gartner style research has repeatedly put "no decision" as the outcome in a HUGE share of qualified opportunities, larger than any single competitor's win rate. <br/>you're usually losing to inertia<br/>the reason is loss aversion, one of the most replicated findings in behavioral science. <br/>people feel the pain of a loss roughly twice as hard as the pleasure of an equivalent gain<br/>run that through the buyer's head. switching risks a visible, personal loss if it goes wrong, the kind their boss remembers at review time. <br/>staying put feels safe EVEN WHEN the status quo bleeds them every month, because the cost of doing nothing is invisible and spread out while the cost of a bad yes is sharp and has their name on it...<br/>so your pitch about upside is fighting a brain hardwired to fear the downside twice as loudly. <br/>this is why why hammering harder on features does nothing. <br/>you're basically adding weight to the side of the scale that was already winning lol <br/>while the side that actually controls the decision, perceived risk, sits UNTOUCHED<br/>## <br/>buying is a group decision, and groups freeze:<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKd9TotW0AAb7Wo?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/DinScales26/article/2064758924369166403/media/2064756730471305216)<br/>real b2b purchases run through committees. <br/>six, eight, sometimes a dozen stakeholders, each with their own risk tolerance, their own boss, their own quiet reason to delay<br/>every person you add multiplies the odds that one of them says "let's revisit next quarter." consensus is fragile. <br/>it only takes ONE nervous voice to stall the whole thing<br/>nobody on a buying committee wants to be the person who championed the thing that failed. <br/>the upside of a good call gets shared across the group, but the downside of a bad one lands on whoever pushed it<br/>so the rational individual move is to just hedge and stay quiet. <br/>multiply that across eight people and you get a room full of professionals all silently incentivized not to move<br/>this is why deals that feel done suddenly go quiet. a SINGLE stakeholder got nervous and pulled the brake<br/>a few smaller biases compound it. ambiguity aversion means anything vague in your pitch quietly reads as risk and pushes them toward doing nothing. <br/>anchoring means the first number they hear frames everything after it<br/>and social proof cuts both ways: if a buyer's peers haven't moved, inaction feels validated. but if you show that companies exactly like them already made this exact decision and it worked, you flip that same instinct onto YOUR SIDE<br/>## <br/>how to actually m</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 11 · 🔁 2 · 💬 2 · 🔖 29</p><p><a href="https://x.com/DinScales26/status/2064758924369166403">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
