## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKet_y2WwAAVaul?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/cobi%5Fbean/article/2064848575708922093/media/2064810265665781760) How my Hermes agent remembers me [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2050065487942369280/gRHqAkQp_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/cobi%5Fbean) [cobi](http://x.com/cobi%5Fbean) [@cobi\_bean](http://x.com/cobi%5Fbean) · 17h...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>cobi</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/cobi_bean">@cobi_bean</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064770509569437696">http://x.com/i/article/2064770509569437696</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064770509569437696">http://x.com/i/article/2064770509569437696</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKet_y2WwAAVaul?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/cobi%5Fbean/article/2064848575708922093/media/2064810265665781760)<br/>How my Hermes agent remembers me<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2050065487942369280/gRHqAkQp_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/cobi%5Fbean)<br/>[cobi](http://x.com/cobi%5Fbean)<br/>[@cobi\_bean](http://x.com/cobi%5Fbean)<br/>·<br/>17h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow cobi\_bean<br/>6<br/>6<br/>54<br/>[26K](http://x.com/cobi%5Fbean/status/2064848575708922093/analytics)<br/>## <br/>why i'm getting more serious about memory plugins for agents<br/>i run hermes agents on my own server.<br/>when i open a new session, my content agent already knows my voice rules, my red lines, what i rejected last week, and what i'm building toward.<br/>that is the part that still feels wild to me. the agent does not start from zero every time i open the terminal.<br/>it remembers enough to keep working.<br/>i've been digging into agent memory systems because i think this is one of the more important layers people are going to care about soon. <br/>the useful version is simple: <br/>&gt; my agent can carry context across real work without making me re-explain the same project every morning.<br/>some quick context: <br/>i'm not married to one memory layer. <br/>i like testing new agent tools early, especially when they touch the parts that actually matter: cost trust recall boundaries ...and whether the thing still works after the demo.<br/>## <br/>every memory system is basically trying to do the same thing<br/>the simple version:<br/>1. something happens in a session<br/>2. the memory layer decides what is worth keeping<br/>3. it saves that context somewhere<br/>4. later, your agent asks for the useful stuff back<br/>5. the useful stuff gets pulled into the next session<br/>6. you need some way to correct it when it gets weird<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKfNHc8WgAAFgrI?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/cobi%5Fbean/article/2064848575708922093/media/2064844482084765696)<br/>the basic loop behind agent memory<br/>the interesting part is what each system thinks a memory should be.<br/>is it a saved fact?<br/>a searchable note?<br/>a summary?<br/>a model of a person?<br/>a model of a project?<br/>that choice changes everything.<br/>## <br/>the cool part: memory can follow the work<br/>this is the part i care about most.<br/>a good memory layer should not be trapped inside one app. if i'm using multiple agentic tools, i want the context to travel with the work.<br/>right now i have Honcho in a few of my hermes agents. i also have it wired into one project inside codex.<br/>so when i'm building an app or tool, the project does not feel completely cold every time i come back.<br/>it can remember the design direction, the weird bug i already chased, the constraints i keep repeating, and the decisions i do not want to relitigate.<br/>that's the category i care about: portable working memory for agents.<br/>what i'm hunting for is memory that can follow a project across sessions and tools.<br/>## <br/>mem0 feels like a memory database for agents<br/>mem0 is one version of this idea.<br/>[![Square profile picture](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2038726039153881088/b3jpb6xu_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/mem0ai)<br/>mem0<br/>@mem0ai<br/>·<br/>[Jun 2](http://x.com/mem0ai/status/2061822612398014782)<br/>![Article cover image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ0QXlyaoAA36lE?format=jpg&amp;name=small)<br/> Article<br/>State of Memory in Agent Harness<br/>Agent harnesses are where AI software actually runs. Cursor, Devin, Claude Code, Codex: these environments handle context, orchestrate tools, coordinate agents, and increasingly, manage memory. The...<br/>27<br/>118<br/>794<br/>[200K](http://x.com/mem0ai/status/2061822612398014782/analytics)<br/>the way i think about it:your agent can save useful details from a conversation, then search for them later when it needs context.<br/>that's a very practical shape. <br/>if you're building an app or agent that needs to remember users, preferenc</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 71 · 🔁 6 · 💬 7 · 🔖 133</p><p><a href="https://x.com/cobi_bean/status/2064848575708922093">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
