## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKdmlNSWoAER-UY?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/dashboardlim/article/2064736540757012991/media/2064731743580495873) What Is Loop Engineering? (And Why It's the Future of AI) [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1983099742784532480/ri6WHAQH_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/dashboardlim) [Lian Lim | Dashboard &amp; AI Automation...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Lian Lim | Dashboard &amp; AI Automation Expert</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/dashboardlim">@dashboardlim</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064729598328184832">http://x.com/i/article/2064729598328184832</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064729598328184832">http://x.com/i/article/2064729598328184832</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKdmlNSWoAER-UY?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/dashboardlim/article/2064736540757012991/media/2064731743580495873)<br/>What Is Loop Engineering? (And Why It's the Future of AI) <br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1983099742784532480/ri6WHAQH_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/dashboardlim)<br/>[Lian Lim | Dashboard &amp; AI Automation Expert](http://x.com/dashboardlim)<br/>[@dashboardlim](http://x.com/dashboardlim)<br/>·<br/>20h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow dashboardlim<br/>1<br/>24<br/>94<br/>[7K](http://x.com/dashboardlim/status/2064736540757012991/analytics)<br/>Loop engineering is designing automated systems that prompt your AI agents instead of you prompting them directly.<br/>You build a small system that finds work, hands it to agents, checks the output, records what's done, and decides what's next.<br/>Then you let that system run while you sleep.<br/>The old way: you type a prompt, read the response, type the next prompt.<br/>The new way: you design the loop once and the loop does the prompting for you.<br/>A few days ago, Boris Cherny said something that made this click for a lot of people.<br/>Cherny is the head of Claude Code at Anthropic.<br/>The guy who built the coding agent that now writes 4% of all GitHub commits.<br/>Here's what he said:<br/>"I don't prompt Claude anymore. I have loops running that prompt Claude and figuring out what to do. My job is to write loops."<br/>That statement went viral.<br/>700,000 views in 24 hours.<br/>Because it named something people were already sensing but couldn't articulate.<br/>The leverage point has moved.<br/>Prompt engineering was the skill of 2024.<br/>Loop engineering is the skill of 2026.<br/># <br/>The Progression<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKdpRZHWwAAkOcJ?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/dashboardlim/article/2064736540757012991/media/2064734701693091840)<br/>Three years ago, the skill was typing fast.<br/>Two years ago, the skill was prompting well.<br/>One year ago, the skill was running multiple agents in parallel.<br/>Now?<br/>The skill is designing the loop that does all of that without you.<br/>The unit of work moved from the keystroke, to the prompt, to the loop.<br/>Most people are still on step two.<br/>The people building the future are already on step four.<br/># <br/>The Five Building Blocks<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKdo2WCWQAAIkh4?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/dashboardlim/article/2064736540757012991/media/2064734237010313216)<br/>Every loop needs five things and a place to remember stuff.<br/>Here's the framework.<br/>## <br/>Automations<br/>The heartbeat of the loop.<br/>Scheduled tasks that run on their own.<br/>Discovery and triage without you.<br/>The loop wakes up, finds work, and acts on it while you sleep.<br/>An automation can run every morning, scan your issue tracker, summarize failures, surface what needs attention, and write the findings to a state file.<br/>You wake up to a prioritized list, not an inbox you have to manually process.<br/>## <br/>Worktrees<br/>Isolation so parallel agents don't collide.<br/>Two agents writing the same file is chaos.<br/>Worktrees give each agent its own branch, its own working directory, sharing the same history.<br/>One agent's work cannot touch another's.<br/>This is what makes parallelism possible without everything breaking.<br/>## <br/>Skills<br/>Project knowledge that survives across runs.<br/>Without skills, every loop run is day one.<br/>The agent forgets everything between sessions.<br/>Skills hold the institutional knowledge.<br/>The conventions, the build steps, the context that would otherwise get lost.<br/>Written once, read every run.<br/>## <br/>Connectors<br/>The loop needs to touch your real tools.<br/>Open PRs, update tickets, post to Slack, query databases, trigger actions.<br/>A loop that can only read files is a loop that can only suggest.<br/>A loop with connectors is a loop that operates.<br/>## <br/>Sub-agents<br/>The agent that wrote the output is a poor judge of its own work.<br/>One agent explores</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 125 · 🔁 29 · 💬 1 · 🔖 184</p><p><a href="https://x.com/dashboardlim/status/2064736540757012991">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
