## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKePXPbbkAAEn9s?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/MaxBrodeurUrbas/article/2064776992403399113/media/2064776583614009344) the 7 deadly sins of bloated AI spend [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1998295391721361409/pYhBqohs_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/MaxBrodeurUrbas) [Max Brodeur-...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Max Brodeur-Urbas</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/MaxBrodeurUrbas">@MaxBrodeurUrbas</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064776326280773632">http://x.com/i/article/2064776326280773632</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064776326280773632">http://x.com/i/article/2064776326280773632</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKePXPbbkAAEn9s?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/MaxBrodeurUrbas/article/2064776992403399113/media/2064776583614009344)<br/>the 7 deadly sins of bloated AI spend<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1998295391721361409/pYhBqohs_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/MaxBrodeurUrbas)<br/>[Max Brodeur-Urbas![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2033696970808954880/7bh9EXRQ_bigger.jpg)](http://x.com/MaxBrodeurUrbas)<br/>[@MaxBrodeurUrbas](http://x.com/MaxBrodeurUrbas)<br/>·<br/>7h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow MaxBrodeurUrbas<br/>4<br/>7<br/>46<br/>[5.5K](http://x.com/MaxBrodeurUrbas/status/2064776992403399113/analytics)<br/>I haven’t read the bible but I’ve read hundreds of Gong call transcripts and spent \~500 hours this year on calls with companies adopting agents.<br/>The biggest issue most AI-forward companies have right now is their spend ballooning across their AI tools to the point where the CFO is slamming the panic button.<br/>The patterns are so consistent that I’ve been calling them sins. For each sin I'll give you the penance. Some penances are strategic, some are a bit more technical but regardless, they will all help you flatten your exponentially growing AI bill while rolling out agents.<br/>1\. Gluttony: Using frontier intelligence for everything.<br/>This is the most talked about sin but it’s shocking how expensive it is at scale. It’s responsible for one or two zeroes on the end of the average company's AI bill.<br/>The world has yet to wake up to the fact that open source models are finally good and unbelievably cheap. I mean unbelievable in the most literal sense. When an engineer told me our internal agents switched to Kimi k2.6 from Opus at an 80% discount without anyone noticing, I made him double check. He’d undersold it, it was 93% cheaper. Open source token prices read like typos.<br/>Using frontier models for everything is lazy and can lead to unjustifiable AI bills.<br/>Solutions:<br/>* Open source models†† must be an option in all agent harnesses. They’re harder to work with but all of those shortcomings can be worked around by whoever built the agent. There will be a massive migration to cheaper inference for most knowledge work soon.<br/>* Model routing† should be available by default. Users are unlikely to switch off the default model, so if you make your default model a blend of multiple levels of intelligence through routing, you see instant savings.<br/>* Subagents†† powered by cheap models are essential to do scalable work. They’re an affordable pressure valve that a smart model can use to alleviate an overwhelming burden.<br/>(From here on out, I'm making up my own deadly sins fyi)<br/>2\. Cowardice: Lacking the courage to optimize agents once they’re working.<br/>Once an agent is working, we’ve noticed how scared anyone is to touch it. The thought of having to work through regressions as you chase improvements is scary enough that people would rather let things be inefficient. This fear is natural but too expensive to tolerate.<br/>Solution:<br/>* Engineer in courage†: All agents need a way to preview potential improvements. People need to see with their own eyes that a cheaper model would have worked before they will believe it. Without this preview, building confidence to optimize requires a literal hero.<br/>* Cost Previews†: The easiest way to motivate optimization is to show “what could have been”. Seeing that you’d have saved thousands of dollars this month with minor improvements is often enough inspiration to have someone make a change. Cost needs to be front and center across the product. It should matter to everyone, not just the CFO.<br/>3\. Blindness: Not being able to track and attribute spend<br/>Most companies have little to no idea where their money is actually going or how valuable the work that was done with those tokens was. Tracing back the impact per token is a multi-million dollar riddle.<br/>Who is spending,</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 56 · 🔁 7 · 💬 5 · 🔖 75</p><p><a href="https://x.com/MaxBrodeurUrbas/status/2064776992403399113">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
