## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKdKIBobEAAPIzO?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/akshay%5Fpachaar/article/2064700531600458093/media/2064700455910051840) Training an LLM to Generate Reliable Structured Output [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1578327351544360960/YFpWSWIX_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/akshay%5Fpachaar) [Akshay...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Akshay 🚀</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/akshay_pachaar">@akshay_pachaar</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064625044308008960">http://x.com/i/article/2064625044308008960</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064625044308008960">http://x.com/i/article/2064625044308008960</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKdKIBobEAAPIzO?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/akshay%5Fpachaar/article/2064700531600458093/media/2064700455910051840)<br/>Training an LLM to Generate Reliable Structured Output <br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1578327351544360960/YFpWSWIX_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/akshay%5Fpachaar)<br/>[Akshay ![🚀](https://abs.twimg.com/emoji/v2/svg/1f680.svg)](http://x.com/akshay%5Fpachaar)<br/>[@akshay\_pachaar](http://x.com/akshay%5Fpachaar)<br/>·<br/>12h<br/>Blocked<br/>6<br/>26<br/>168<br/>[16K](http://x.com/akshay%5Fpachaar/status/2064700531600458093/analytics)<br/>You ask a language model for JSON, and most of the time it works.<br/>Then one response comes back broken. A number shows up as a string, or the model wraps the JSON in a line of explanation, and the parser chokes.<br/>That small failure rate is the whole problem. The output feeds a function call or a database write, and looking like valid JSON is not the same as being valid JSON.<br/>The code downstream only finds out which one it got when it breaks.<br/>That is what structured output means. The model returns data in a fixed shape that matches a schema, not free text that happens to read right.<br/>Agents, tool calls, and data pipelines all run on this now. The model is writing for code to run, not for a person to read.<br/>Getting this reliable is the hard part, and the usual answer is to give the model more. You add examples, tighten the prompt, and fine-tune on correct outputs.<br/>It helps a little, then stops. The ceiling was never the amount of data. It is the objective.<br/>DeepSeek-R1 showed a way around this. Training a strong model used to mean annotation pipelines, preference pairs, and a team of labelers.<br/>DeepSeek replaced all of it with one Python function that checks whether an answer is right. If you can define correctness via code, you do not need the rest.<br/>That is the idea behind GRPO. Instead of learning from examples, the model learns from a reward function you write.<br/>For each prompt, it generates a few candidate answers. The reward function scores them, and the model is pushed toward the ones that score higher.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKcGTQbbIAAvvOz?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/akshay%5Fpachaar/article/2064700531600458093/media/2064625882069934080)<br/>GRPO explained step by step, from data prep to response generation, reward calculation and loss calculation <br/>In this walkthrough, I use it to fine-tune Qwen3-8B for JSON extraction. The loop runs from a local notebook while the model trains on remote H200s.<br/>The reward function does one thing: it checks whether each output parses and matches the schema.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKcGHaYaUAAnYMy?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/akshay%5Fpachaar/article/2064700531600458093/media/2064625678583222272)<br/>The whole loop is driven from your local Python process, while the sampling and gradient steps run on the remote H200s.<br/>Schema-valid output went from 62% on the base model to 82% after training, past GPT-4.1 on the same eval at 58%.<br/>Before the build, it helps to see why the obvious approach stalls. That is what makes everything after it click.<br/># <br/>Why SFT Hits a Ceiling<br/>SFT learns by copying examples. Show it correct completions, and it gets good at producing output that looks like them.<br/>But looking like valid JSON and being valid JSON are different goals. SFT only ever chases the first one.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKcRexvaMAABWaV?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/akshay%5Fpachaar/article/2064700531600458093/media/2064638174618595328)<br/>At the token level, a completion with one wrong field type scores almost the same as a valid one. More data lifts schema accuracy to a ceiling, then stops.<br/>The loss is measured token by token. A completion with one field typed wrong scores almost the same as a perfect one.<br/>So you add more examples. T</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 244 · 🔁 35 · 💬 9 · 🔖 347</p><p><a href="https://x.com/akshay_pachaar/status/2064700531600458093">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
