## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKeIAoXbIAECCVr?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/aparnadhinak/article/2064768757713768507/media/2064768498589704193) The Plan Is Code: Trying Out Claude Code's Dynamic Workflows [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1258117513872728064/c5A0LbF5_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/aparnadhinak) [Aparna...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Aparna Dhinakaran</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/aparnadhinak">@aparnadhinak</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064764378357059584">http://x.com/i/article/2064764378357059584</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064764378357059584">http://x.com/i/article/2064764378357059584</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKeIAoXbIAECCVr?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/aparnadhinak/article/2064768757713768507/media/2064768498589704193)<br/>The Plan Is Code: Trying Out Claude Code's Dynamic Workflows<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1258117513872728064/c5A0LbF5_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/aparnadhinak)<br/>[Aparna Dhinakaran![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2051433378692640768/Ph-ysq0G_bigger.jpg)](http://x.com/aparnadhinak)<br/>[@aparnadhinak](http://x.com/aparnadhinak)<br/>·<br/>7h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow aparnadhinak<br/>1<br/>6<br/>78<br/>[6.1K](http://x.com/aparnadhinak/status/2064768757713768507/analytics)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKeGEZAakAA_U3p?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/aparnadhinak/article/2064768757713768507/media/2064766364162887680)<br/>Dynamic Workflows are Claude Code's newest way of coordinating agents. We have followed the various industry approaches to orchestration and will continue to highlight unique ideas we see in the space.<br/>Agent orchestration is a growing area of research focused on the question: How do I organize a group of agents, each with specialization, to accomplish a task? The core areas of differentiation follow in these areas:<br/>* Agent Specialization - what roles do the agents take on<br/>* Agent Role Assignment - are the roles fixed or dynamic<br/>* Context Sharing - how are results shared<br/>* Orchestration - who starts, stops or removes agents<br/>* Review and Test - how is review and test handled<br/>Dynamic workflows present a unique orchestration technique that looks to be designed for a certain type of task: one that is parallelizable and that requires a high quality final answer.<br/>The first big difference between what Claude Dynamic workers does and what we would call typical orchestration is that Dynamic Workflows turns a plan into code. It writes code that contains an agent orchestration loop.<br/>Before dynamic workflows, every multi-agent setup we’ve seen maintained the plan inside a conversation. A single agent held it in its own context window. An orchestrator gathered subagent results back into its window. Agent teams passed a shared task list around. The bigger the job, the more the conversation could drown the orchestrator in its own coordination.<br/>A script at the center spawns the agents that do the work: each task gets a dynamically created set of roles. The finder that surfaces candidate issues, a verifier that tries to tear them down, a fixer to make changes, and a synthesizer that folds the survivors into one answer. The results come back as values in the script's variables rather than as messages between agents, so you only ever see the final report.<br/>We wanted to give it a solid task so we pointed it at a 30,000-file production codebase and told it to hunt for security bugs. It took thirty-two minutes, 330 subagents, and 22 million tokens to output a short list of issues. What follows is how a run like that works, and what we found when we went back and verified its findings.<br/>## <br/>What it’s made of<br/>You only need to understand three building blocks of Dynamic Workflows to follow our run:<br/>* agent() spawns one subagent and returns its text, or, with a schema, validated structured data.<br/>* parallel() fans several agents out at once and waits for all of them to finish before it returns.<br/>* pipeline() also fans out, but doesn't wait, so one item can be three stages along while another is still on its first.<br/>Two limits sit underneath: at most sixteen agents run at once (less with a weaker machine), with the rest queued until a slot frees, and no single run may exceed a thousand agents in total, which is a backstop against a loop that never terminates.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKeGO4LbgAALZv2?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/aparnadhinak/article/2064768757713768507/media/2064766544329277440)<br/>The diagram contrasts the two. A p</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 317 · 🔁 33 · 💬 6 · 🔖 521</p><p><a href="https://x.com/aparnadhinak/status/2064768757713768507">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
