## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKd3darboAcMVA_?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/alexeheath/article/2064751005380878379/media/2064750301434060807) Inside Apple's Siri revamp [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1983378648787988480/cGjEMRvw_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/alexeheath) [Alex Heath](http://x.com/alexeheath) [@alexeheath](http://x.com/alexeheath) · 8h Follow...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Alex Heath</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/alexeheath">@alexeheath</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064750162594209794">http://x.com/i/article/2064750162594209794</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064750162594209794">http://x.com/i/article/2064750162594209794</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKd3darboAcMVA_?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/alexeheath/article/2064751005380878379/media/2064750301434060807)<br/>Inside Apple's Siri revamp<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1983378648787988480/cGjEMRvw_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/alexeheath)<br/>[Alex Heath](http://x.com/alexeheath)<br/>[@alexeheath](http://x.com/alexeheath)<br/>·<br/>8h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow alexeheath<br/>3<br/>10<br/>78<br/>[17K](http://x.com/alexeheath/status/2064751005380878379/analytics)<br/>Right after Apple’s WWDC keynote finished, I joined dozens of other journalists, influencers, and analysts in a dimly lit theater at Apple Park to hear an AI 101 class and Q&amp;A with Craig Federighi and other Apple execs. Tim Cook and incoming CEO John Ternus sat in the front row.<br/>Apple has never done an on-the-record press Q&amp;A like this after a big keynote. There was no live broadcasting, but below are my notes and takeaways:<br/>## <br/>The Google deal, decoded<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKd3kHWa8AAgwDe?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/alexeheath/article/2064751005380878379/media/2064750416504745984)<br/>To start, Federighi clicked to a slide of a “traditional chatbot architecture” and said the amount of Google’s consumer assistant inside Apple’s system is “none.” No Gemini app, no Google-served models, no Google Search for grounding.<br/>What Apple runs instead for the new Siri is a <br/>[new family of five Apple Foundation Models](https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/introducing-third-generation-of-apple-foundation-models)<br/>:<br/>* On device: AFM 3 Core (3B, dense) and Core Advanced (20B sparse, firing just 1–4B parameters per request to power the new voices and dictation).<br/>* On Apple’s Private Cloud Compute stack: AFM 3 Cloud (the workhorse), ADM 3 Cloud (images and Spatial Reframing), and AFM 3 Cloud Pro (the heavyweight, for agentic tool use and complex reasoning).<br/>Google touches three of those steps, not one: its frontier Gemini models refine Apple’s models, Apple trains on Google’s cloud TPUs, and Cloud Pro runs on Nvidia GPUs inside Google Cloud.<br/>Apple hasn’t put an Nvidia GPU in a Mac in about a decade, after a defect debacle and a Jobs-era falling-out. Now, its most intense Siri reasoning runs on Nvidia silicon inside Google’s data center, wrapped in an extended Private Cloud Compute that Apple says is non-negotiable. We live in interesting times.<br/>## <br/>Not a chatbot or agent<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKd3oG8boAMIiDh?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/alexeheath/article/2064751005380878379/media/2064750485115215875)<br/>Federighi insisted Siri isn’t “a separate chatbot, an unintegrated place you go and chit chat,” but something you summon over anything on an iPhone screen.<br/>When answering a submitted question agents, he quipped to the room that “you guys obviously watched I/O,” which drew laughs. He called today’s agents “exciting experiments” but “very early days.”<br/>Mike Rockwell, who now runs Siri, did jump in to say that that Siri’s new “modern architecture” gives Apple “the ability to extend it in the future.” For now, though, Apple isn’t sweating the agent boom; the open-source agent crowd is already buying Mac Minis to run their OpenClaws.<br/>## <br/>OpenAI is on the outs<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKd3vPcboAAuDN1?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/alexeheath/article/2064751005380878379/media/2064750607656001536)<br/>Apple has clearly cooled on OpenAI. From my conversations at Apple today, it’s clear that its leaders sees Google as the more mature partner to essentially bet the model underpinnings of Siri on.<br/>The ChatGPT product hand-off isn’t dead in that you’ll still be able to ask the new Siri to route a query to ChatGPT, but it’s opt-in and won’t be a flow that sends OpenAI real traffic.<br/>OpenAI filed confidentially for an IPO this afternoon, is building hardware with Jony Iv</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 108 · 🔁 11 · 💬 4 · 🔖 124</p><p><a href="https://x.com/alexeheath/status/2064751005380878379">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
