## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKeQnxWWUAIZtnN?format=png&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/AgnoAgi/article/2064780623026540669/media/2064777967109033986) The Framework Wars Are Over, and Nobody Won [![Square profile picture](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1984361332624306176/KaNuKvU4_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/AgnoAgi) [Agno](http://x.com/AgnoAgi)...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Agno</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/AgnoAgi">@AgnoAgi</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064777704147144704">http://x.com/i/article/2064777704147144704</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064777704147144704">http://x.com/i/article/2064777704147144704</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKeQnxWWUAIZtnN?format=png&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/AgnoAgi/article/2064780623026540669/media/2064777967109033986)<br/>The Framework Wars Are Over, and Nobody Won<br/>[![Square profile picture](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1984361332624306176/KaNuKvU4_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/AgnoAgi)<br/>[Agno](http://x.com/AgnoAgi)<br/>[@AgnoAgi](http://x.com/AgnoAgi)<br/>·<br/>6h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow AgnoAgi<br/>1<br/>5<br/>[196](http://x.com/AgnoAgi/status/2064780623026540669/analytics)<br/>Agent frameworks and agent runtimes are two different jobs that got bundled by accident They write decision docs. Run bake-offs. Commit. Then six months later, the field has moved and they have the conversation again.<br/>The comparison keeps breaking down because the word "framework" papers over two separate jobs:<br/>Orchestration is how the agent reasons, calls tools, and composes sub-agents. This is the layer that holds the actual ideas about how an agent should think. It benefits from competition and iteration. New patterns show up every few months, and they should.<br/>Runtime is how the agent runs in production. HTTP endpoints, sessions, auth, streaming, tracing, scaling, governance. Every team solves roughly the same problem here, and adjacent domains solved it more than a decade ago.<br/>Most early agent frameworks bundled both. That bundling is what teams are paying for now.<br/>## <br/>The cost of bundling<br/>It shows up in three places:<br/>Switching costs balloon for no good reason. Changing how a team expresses agent logic should be a question about ergonomics and fit. Instead it becomes a re-platforming project because the deployment surface ships with the orchestration code.<br/>Operational patterns fragment. Every framework models sessions slightly differently. Every framework streams slightly differently. Every framework integrates auth slightly differently. Multiply the differences by the number of agents a team runs.<br/>The runtime lags the orchestration. Frameworks ship new reasoning patterns fast and new production patterns slowly, because production isn't the interesting part of the project. Teams end up forking the runtime to get JWT support, or rate limiting, or whatever they need next.<br/>## <br/>What would "winning" have looked like?<br/>The phrase "framework wars" assumes one contender should pull ahead and become the standard. That outcome is neither likely nor desirable.<br/>The orchestration layer is where agent engineering is still inventing itself. The field needs many frameworks. It needs sharp opinions. It needs the framework that ships next year to beat the one that shipped last year, and it needs teams to move to the new one cheaply.<br/>What the field does not need is for that movement to require rebuilding the production layer.<br/>## <br/>The test<br/>Imagine your team picked the wrong orchestration tool eighteen months ago. What does the migration look like?<br/>If the answer involves reimplementing sessions, reimplementing streaming, reimplementing auth, and reimplementing tracing, the architecture needs rethinking, not the framework choice.<br/>If the answer is "rewrite the agent against a different SDK and redeploy," the architecture is right, no matter which framework the team happened to pick.<br/>## <br/>How AgentOS separates the two<br/>AgentOS is a framework-agnostic runtime. Your team writes the agent in whatever orchestration tool makes sense. AgentOS handles the production surface underneath: APIs, sessions, streaming, scale, auth, observability, and governance.<br/>The orchestration choice stays contestable. The runtime choice stops mattering.<br/>The framework wars were never the real fight. The fight is whether your production layer survives the next three frameworks.<br/>[Read the full blog](https://www.agno.com/blog/the-framework-wars-are-over-and-nobody-won?utm%5Fsource=twitter&amp;utm%5Fmedium=post&amp;utm%5Fcampaign=tl&amp;utm%5Fcontent=tl-</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 6 · 🔁 1 · 🔖 9</p><p><a href="https://x.com/AgnoAgi/status/2064780623026540669">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
