## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKZBqp2a4AAwf2d?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/lotte%5Fverheyden/article/2064415370761646410/media/2064409680240369664) AI is eating the AI Engineering Loop [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1994707418790432768/NvTWenyx_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/lotte%5Fverheyden)...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Lotte</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/lotte_verheyden">@lotte_verheyden</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064400841029910528">http://x.com/i/article/2064400841029910528</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064400841029910528">http://x.com/i/article/2064400841029910528</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKZBqp2a4AAwf2d?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/lotte%5Fverheyden/article/2064415370761646410/media/2064409680240369664)<br/>AI is eating the AI Engineering Loop<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1994707418790432768/NvTWenyx_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/lotte%5Fverheyden)<br/>[Lotte![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2044396073561550848/qCtNGUmN_bigger.jpg)](http://x.com/lotte%5Fverheyden)<br/>[@lotte\_verheyden](http://x.com/lotte%5Fverheyden)<br/>·<br/>16h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow lotte\_verheyden<br/>2<br/>21<br/>92<br/>[9.5K](http://x.com/lotte%5Fverheyden/status/2064415370761646410/analytics)<br/>The full AI engineering loop can technically be automated now. But that doesn't mean it should. Here is what we think you should hand to agents, and what you should keep doing yourself.<br/>AI agents can run almost every step of the <br/>[AI engineering loop](http://127.0.0.1:3334/blog/2026-06-03-ai-is-eating-ai-engineering#ai-engineering-loop)<br/> on their own. The tools are in place, the context is available, and the loop can close without a human touching it.<br/>That is also where the industry is heading, often referred to as "continual learning":<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1867875781676007424/RIF4Kt7U_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/swyx)<br/>swyx<br/>@swyx<br/>·<br/>[May 31](http://x.com/swyx/status/2061206120233054327)<br/>every evals/analytics startup is going through a onetime generational upgrade into a continual learning platform in 2026 many will fail but as always the tasteful ones win<br/>69<br/>45<br/>300<br/>[39K](http://x.com/swyx/status/2061206120233054327/analytics)<br/>We think this is directionally right. We also think handing over the whole loop is a mistake. Automate past the point where you can still vouch for the output, and you'll find yourself shipping agent slop.<br/># <br/>What do we mean by AI engineering loop?<br/>The AI engineering loop is how we describe the process of continuously improving AI agents, based on what we have seen across the industry and across our user base. We wrote about it extensively in our <br/>[academy](http://127.0.0.1:3334/academy/ai-engineering-loop)<br/>.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKY6ZoQboAAeXef?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/lotte%5Fverheyden/article/2064415370761646410/media/2064401691173429248)<br/>Part of the loop runs on live activity. Traces flow in from production, and monitoring surfaces anything worth a closer look. Part of monitoring is reading traces yourself. This gives you direct insight into how the system behaves and is one of the more valuable steps in the whole process.<br/>The rest happens in development, before shipping a change. You build a dataset that approximates real production usage, so you can test changes systematically and hill-climb on quality. If a change performs better, you deploy it to production. This is a continuous process.<br/># <br/>You could automate all of it<br/>Walk through the steps and none of them technically need a human. Instrumenting an app is something agents do fully autonomously now. For every other step, platforms usually have an <br/>[API](http://127.0.0.1:3334/docs/api-and-data-platform/features/public-api)<br/> or a <br/>[CLI](http://127.0.0.1:3334/docs/api-and-data-platform/features/cli)<br/> an agent can call to do the work.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKY6jyka8AAfuIm?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/lotte%5Fverheyden/article/2064415370761646410/media/2064401865740316672)<br/>So if every step can be automated, the loop can close on its own. But while we can technically automate the entire process, that does not mean we should. Removing yourself from the loop comes at a cost, and that cost has a name: agent slop.<br/># <br/>But you'd be producing agent slop<br/>&gt; Agent slop: low-quality AI agents, mass produced by other AI agents. Often the result of agents optimizing against imperfect evals and datasets.<br/>You want your agen</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 126 · 🔁 18 · 💬 2 · 🔖 193</p><p><a href="https://x.com/lotte_verheyden/status/2064415370761646410">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>@PawelHuryn: Six patterns for building dynamic workflows and loops identified by Anthropic: 1....</title>
      <link>https://x.com/PawelHuryn/status/2064201950980096078</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">2064201950980096078</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 09 Jun 2026 04:24:30 GMT</pubDate>
      <dc:creator>Paweł Huryn</dc:creator>
      <description>Six patterns for building dynamic workflows and loops identified by Anthropic:
&gt;
&gt; 1. Classify-and-act: one agent decides the type, the script routes it. Example: bug vs feature vs noise.
&gt;
&gt; 2. Fan-out-and-synthesize: one agent per piece, merged in code. Examples: market research, competitor teardown.
&gt;
&gt; 3. Adversarial verification: a separate agent checks the output against a rubric. Example: fact-checking a PRD against the sources.
&gt;
&gt; 4. Generate-and-filter: many candidates, deduped, the survivors kept. Examples: naming, positioning, ideation.
&gt;
&gt; 5. Tournament (compare): agents attempt the task different ways, judges compare until one wins. Example: product strategy.
&gt;
&gt; 6. Loop-until-done: spawn until a stop condition. Example: implement, document, and test a feature in one shot.

