## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKVb67PbQAA7cFC?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/agent%5Fwrapper/article/2064157228400341312/media/2064157072112173056) What Are Agent Loops, Really? [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2024262820892995584/7VeKiw-o_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/agent%5Fwrapper) [prateek](http://x.com/agent%5Fwrapper)...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>prateek</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/agent_wrapper">@agent_wrapper</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064149340298641408">http://x.com/i/article/2064149340298641408</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064149340298641408">http://x.com/i/article/2064149340298641408</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKVb67PbQAA7cFC?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/agent%5Fwrapper/article/2064157228400341312/media/2064157072112173056)<br/>What Are Agent Loops, Really?<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2024262820892995584/7VeKiw-o_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/agent%5Fwrapper)<br/>[prateek](http://x.com/agent%5Fwrapper)<br/>[@agent\_wrapper](http://x.com/agent%5Fwrapper)<br/>·<br/>10h<br/>·<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow agent\_wrapper<br/>3<br/>10<br/>79<br/>[7.9K](http://x.com/agent%5Fwrapper/status/2064157228400341312/analytics)<br/>Agent "loops" are everywhere right now. Everyone from <br/>[@steipete](https://x.com/@steipete)<br/> to <br/>[@ThePrimeagen](https://x.com/@ThePrimeagen)<br/> has been talking about them.<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1131851609774985216/OcsssQ9J_normal.png)](http://x.com/steipete)<br/>Peter Steinberger ![🦞](https://abs.twimg.com/emoji/v2/svg/1f99e.svg)<br/>![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2017100664984186880/orbYx-3U_bigger.jpg)<br/>@steipete<br/>·<br/>[Jun 7](http://x.com/steipete/status/2063697162748260627)<br/>Here’s your monthly reminder that you shouldn’t be prompting coding agents anymore. You should be designing loops that prompt your agents.<br/>1.6K<br/>2.5K<br/>18K<br/>[6.9M](http://x.com/steipete/status/2063697162748260627/analytics)<br/>In this article, I've tried to condense my understanding of agent loops based on building one of the most popular open source Agent IDEs. It's called Agent Orchestrator (<br/>[@aoagents](https://x.com/@aoagents)<br/>), and currently has ⭐7.5K Stars and \~ 1K forks on Github. <br/>[@aoagents](https://x.com/@aoagents)<br/> gives you most of these loops out of the box, without lifting a finger.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKVZe3YaIAAfJn4?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/agent%5Fwrapper/article/2064157228400341312/media/2064154391016513536)<br/>Here's the thing most of that conversation is circling: prompting a coding agent one turn at a time is not the job. If you're hand-feeding an agent each instruction, reading its output, pasting in the error, and prompting again, you're doing the loop manually — you are the loop. The whole point is to design a system where the agent runs that cycle itself: discovering, executing, observing real results, and correcting until the goal is met.<br/>But what does a real loop actually look like in practice? And what does it take to make loops reliable instead of expensive, noisy, or chaotic?<br/># <br/>What a Loop Actually Is<br/>A loop is nothing exotic. It's the same thing a good developer already does every day:<br/>write code → get real feedback (tests, CI, review) → fix → repeat → and when it's done, pick up the next piece.<br/>That's it. An agent loop is a persistent, iterative, goal-oriented version of that exact cycle:<br/>1. Understand the goal (and hold context around it)<br/>2. Execute (write code, use tools, make changes)<br/>3. Observe (get real feedback from the environment, not the model's own opinion)<br/>4. Reflect &amp; correct (use that feedback to adjust)<br/>5. Repeat until success criteria are met — then move to new work.<br/>The power is that you design the system once and the loop handles the repeated prompting, iteration, and self-correction. That's very different from:<br/>* One-shot prompting<br/>* Long back-and-forth conversations where you stay in the driver’s seat<br/>* Naive “just put the agent in a while(true) loop and hope for the best”<br/>Most people who try to "add loops" end up with expensive agents that mostly agree with themselves or spiral without making real progress. The difference is entirely in what the loop is allowed to push against.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKVW3p0boAA2ZMv?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/agent%5Fwrapper/article/2064157228400341312/media/2064151518337802240)<br/>The basic cycle. Effective loops are powered by real signals from the environment — tests, visual verification, metrics, critics, human judgment, persis</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 143 · 🔁 22 · 💬 7 · 🔖 228</p><p><a href="https://x.com/agent_wrapper/status/2064157228400341312">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
