- GitHub - robzilla1738/supergoal: Plan and autonomously build a software task end-to-end. One ready-to-paste /goal, adaptive phase count, memory preload + writeback, 3-strike self-healing recovery. Works on Claude Code and Codex. · GitHub: https://github.com/robzilla1738/supergoal</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Robert Courson</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/robertcourson">@robertcourson</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064157304472154112">http://x.com/i/article/2064157304472154112</a></p></blockquote><div><img src="https://opengraph.githubassets.com/f070c4249d37085a19669279ae75572d48e2b4a3919775e286ed6295d37fe2ef/robzilla1738/supergoal" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/></div><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2064157304472154112">http://x.com/i/article/2064157304472154112</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKVeeI8WAAAIqDY?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/robertcourson/article/2064161006373216269/media/2064159876108910592)<br/>/Loops, /goals, and long-running agents<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1978481556592160768/bbrbXFde_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/robertcourson)<br/>[Robert Courson](http://x.com/robertcourson)<br/>[@robertcourson](http://x.com/robertcourson)<br/>·<br/>10h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow robertcourson<br/>2<br/>6<br/>64<br/>[4.6K](http://x.com/robertcourson/status/2064161006373216269/analytics)<br/>I’ve been thinking about this a lot while running bigger agent builds recently.<br/>The tasks that finish in a useful state usually don’t succeed because the model is proportionally better. 9.8 times out of 10 it's because the structure around the goal is better.<br/>The failure modes are weirdly consistent. Context gets diluted. One error turns into a restart. Earlier learnings disappear. Verification happens too late, or it doesn’t happen at all. And you end up babysitting every step because there isn’t enough structure holding the work together.<br/>This is where my projects like supergoal and goalkeeper came from. I built them test some ideas around this, but the point is broader than one or two tools.<br/>It’s about what seems to work when you’re trying to keep long-running work from slowly drifting into “mostly done” forever.<br/>## <br/>A goal needs more than a prompt<br/>A high-level goal is useful, but it usually isn’t enough for anything non-trivial.<br/>The system still has to break the work down, remember constraints, track what changed, recover from failures, and decide when something is actually done. If all of that only lives in the conversation, it starts to drift pretty quickly.<br/>What helps is making the work explicit up front. (a contract if you will)<br/>Break it into phases sized to the actual task. Maybe two phases for a small change. Maybe eight or twelve for a bigger build. Each phase should be checkable on its own. You should be able to look at the phase cold and know whether it worked.<br/>The done criteria matter too. “The UI looks better” is not enough. “The build passes,” “this file exists,” “this command exits zero,” “this route returns the right shape” gives the loop something real to check.<br/>That sounds obvious, but this is where a lot of long tasks fall apart. The work feels done for a while, then the next phase reveals that it was only kind of done.<br/>## <br/>The loop matters<br/>The loop that seems to work is simple:<br/>\&gt;Read the current state and the phase spec<br/>\&gt;Do the work<br/>\&gt;Capture evidence<br/>\&gt;Verify the result<br/>\&gt;Save anything non-obvious<br/>\&gt;Move on<br/>The evidence part matters more than I expected. A summary from the agent is not proof. Command output, diffs, file listings, screenshots, test results, whatever shows what actually happened.<br/>Without that, the next phase is trusting vibes.<br/>Verification is where drift starts. If every phase gets checked against something real, the whole task has a much better chance of staying clean. For code, that might mean the normal test/build commands plus cheap checks for obvious junk: debug prints, new TODOs, dead imports, broken formatting, etc.<br/>None of that is fancy. It just catches a surprising amount before it spreads.<br/>## <br/>Recovery should not mean starting over<br/>Long tasks fail (that much is often unavoidable.) The important part is what happens next.<br/>A brittle loop treats failure as terminal or restarts from the beginning. That wastes the work that already succeeded and usually loses the context that would have helped fix it.<br/>A better loop has levels of recovery.<br/>First, retry with the actual failure context.<br/>If that doesn’t work, generate a narrow fix for the thing that failed.<br/>If that still fails, hand off with the full history so a person or another process can step in without reconstructing everything from scratch.<br/>Most failur</blockquote><p>🔗 <a href="https://github.com/robzilla1738/supergoal">GitHub - robzilla1738/supergoal: Plan and autonomously build a software task end-to-end. One ready-to-paste /goal, adaptive phase count, memory preload + writeback, 3-strike self-healing recovery. Works on Claude Code and Codex. · GitHub</a></p><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 123 · 🔁 12 · 💬 3 · 🔖 285</p><p><a href="https://x.com/robertcourson/status/2064161006373216269">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
